{"id":"ce9a35ca-e509-4c57-ae74-d314b46b00ab","object":"chat.completion","created":1789996789,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"schema\": \"amr-supervisor/v1\",\n  \"select\": [\n    {\n      \"when\": \"not OwnerFree\",\n      \"action\": \"Defer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"RequestA and not RequestB\",\n      \"action\": \"SelectA\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"RequestB and not RequestA\",\n      \"action\": \"SelectB\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"RequestA and RequestB and PreferA and not PreferB\",\n      \"action\": \"SelectA\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"RequestA and RequestB and PreferB and not PreferA\",\n      \"action\": \"SelectB\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"RequestA and RequestB\",\n      \"action\": \"SelectA\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"True\",\n      \"action\": \"Defer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"step\": [\n    {\n      \"when\": \"Halted and (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked)\",\n      \"action\": \"Brake\",\n      \"next\": \"Stopped\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"not Halted and (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked)\",\n      \"action\": \"Brake\",\n      \"next\": \"BrakeRequested\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"AtGoal and Released and Halted\",\n      \"action\": \"Finish\",\n      \"next\": \"Done\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"AtGoal and Released and not Halted\",\n      \"action\": \"Brake\",\n      \"next\": \"BrakeRequested\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ\",\n      \"action\": \"Release\",\n      \"next\": \"Traversing\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\",\n      \"action\": \"Brake\",\n      \"next\": \"BrakeRequested\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"not OwnReservation and not Released\",\n      \"action\": \"Request\",\n      \"next\": \"Waiting\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"ModeStopped\",\n      \"action\": \"Resume\",\n      \"next\": \"ResumePending\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"True\",\n      \"action\": \"Proceed\",\n      \"next\": \"Traversing\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We need answer only JSON object. Need synthesize supervisor rules. Need parse policy. Need produce schema, select, step arrays at most 12 each. Need exact expressions allowed. Need ensure all inputs result first true. Need no tools. Need reason carefully.\n\nWe need build select function for dispatcher? Wait \"Policy presentation P3: dispatcher's decision procedure.\" The prompt includes select inputs and step inputs. Need perhaps synthesize both select and step arrays. Select policy? The text after step policy says \"Reservation selection has precisely constraints...\" Need derive select rules satisfying interface. Need select actions SelectA/SelectB/Defer based on OwnerFree, RequestA, RequestB, PreferA, PreferB. Requirements: Selecting A requires OwnerFree and RequestA; selecting B requires OwnerFree and RequestB. If reservation free and at least one request exists, select an eligible robot, rather than Defer. Otherwise Defer required. When both eligible, either legal; preferences don't impose additional acceptance obligation, may conflict/absent. We can use preferences to choose when both? Need ensure if free and at least one request exists, not Defer. For cases:\nOwnerFree false: Defer.\nOwnerFree true:\n- RequestA true, RequestB false: SelectA\n- RequestA false, RequestB true: SelectB\n- both true: either SelectA or SelectB. Need choose maybe using PreferA/PreferB. If PreferA and not PreferB maybe SelectA; if PreferB and not PreferA SelectB; else any. Need rules first true determines result.\n- both false: Defer.\nNeed cover all 32 combos. Max 12. We can write simple ordered rules:\n1 when not OwnerFree -> Defer\n2 when RequestA and not RequestB -> SelectA\n3 when RequestB and not RequestA -> SelectB\n4 when RequestA and RequestB and PreferA and not PreferB -> SelectA\n5 when RequestA and RequestB and PreferB and not PreferA -> SelectB\n6 when RequestA and RequestB -> SelectA (default tie; could ignore prefs conflicting/both absent, but if PreferB true and PreferA false covered rule 5, if PreferA true PreferB true rule 6 select A, if PreferA true PreferB false covered rule4, if both false rule6. If PreferB true PreferA false covered; if PreferB true PreferA true rule6 A. That's legal though doesn't follow conflicting? Preferences advisory, no obligation. But need \"When both robots are eligible, either is legal; preferences do not impose additional acceptance obligation\". So fine. But if RequestA and RequestB and PreferB only, should choose B if we want follow preference but not required. Rule5 does. If PreferA only, rule4. Good.\n7 when True -> Defer (for OwnerFree true but no requests). Wait after rule1 not owner free, rule2/3/4/5/6 cover any OwnerFree true at least one request? Let's check OwnerFree true, RequestA false, RequestB true -> rule3. A true B false -> rule2. Both true -> rules 4-6. Both false -> not covered. Need rule7 True -> Defer covers OwnerFree true no requests. Also covers? all others already covered, no issue. But rule7 True could be after all, okay. Need max. Could also combine not OwnerFree or (not RequestA and not RequestB) -> Defer first? But fine. We need ensure first rule if not OwnerFree Defer, but if OwnerFree true no request eventually Defer. Good. Select expressions use not, and, or allowed. Need no \"True\"? Yes allow True, False.\n\nStep function harder. Need synthesize ordered rules per policy. We have current mode facts and step inputs. Need action and next mode. Need exactly one mode flag true. We can refer to mode flags. Need all 7*256 combos. Need follow normative priority. Need at most 12 rules. Need derive complete decision tree.\n\nLet's restate step policy.\n\nInputs booleans:\nObservationUsable (OU)\nPedestrianBlocked (PB)\nOwnReservation (OR)\nReleased (REL)\nBodyClearOfZ (Body)\nHalted (H)\nAtGoal (Goal)\nTaskActive (TA)\nMode flags exactly one:\nModeIdle (MI)\nModeWaiting (MW)\nModeTraversing (MT)\nModeBrakeRequested (MBR)\nModeStopped (MS)\nModeResumePending (MRP)\nModeDone (MD)\n\nActions: Request, Proceed, Brake, Resume, Release, Finish.\nNext modes: Idle, Waiting, Traversing, BrakeRequested, Stopped, ResumePending, Done.\n\nPolicy step:\n\nAt every update dispatcher first asks whether observation can be used, task remains active, and crossing free of pedestrian block. If any of these three checks fails, instruction is Brake. Stop confirmation then decides next mode: Halted means Stopped, and no Halted means BrakeRequested. No other instruction takes precedence over this instruction.\n\nSo Check1 = not (ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked). Equiv not OU or not TA or PB. If true -> action Brake; next = H if Halted else BrakeRequested. Need split into two rules due next dependent on Halted. Priority first:\nR1 when (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted -> Brake, Stopped.\nR2 when (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) -> Brake, BrakeRequested.\nNeed ensure rule1 first if H true, else rule2. This implements.\n\nOnly when all three checks succeed (OU and TA and not PB) does dispatcher consider released robot at goal. AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done. For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns reservation and has entire body clear of zone must report Release/Traversing. Clear body and released separate.\n\nSo after safe checks ok:\nR3 when AtGoal and Released and Halted -> Finish, Done? Wait text: \"AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done.\" So if AtGoal and Released:\n- if Halted true -> Finish/Done.\n- if Halted false -> Brake/BrakeRequested.\nNeed priority after first check. It says only when checks succeed, consider released robot at goal. AtGoal+Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done. So within this, H decides. Need maybe R3 for Halted -> Finish Done. R4 for AtGoal and Released (not H) -> Brake BrakeRequested. Order? If Halted true, Finish. If Halted false, Brake. Need include safe checks? Since earlier R1/R2 catch not safe. But our rule expressions could not include safe? Because R1/R2 already first and if not safe, they catch. For subsequent rules, we can assume safe? In ordered rules, first true. If we don't include safe in R3, could it trigger for unsafe? No R1/R2 catch unsafe first. For cases Halted? If unsafe and Halted, R1 catches. If unsafe and not H, R2 catches. Thus subsequent rules only reached if safe. So no need to repeat safe in later rules, but can for clarity. However if we need exact priority, okay. But independent evaluator may evaluate ordered; first true. So later rules can omit safe because unsafe cases already true earlier. Good.\n\n\"For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing. Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\"\n\nAfter safe and not (AtGoal & Released)? Actually \"For inputs not handled so far\" means not handled by prior: safe, and not (AtGoal & Released)? Need parse: Only when checks succeed does dispatcher consider a released robot at its goal. AtGoal together with Released requires ... For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns reservation and body clear must Release/Traversing. So after rules for safe + AtGoal&Released (both H and not H?) We need handle AtGoal & Released if Halted -> Finish/Done, if not H -> Brake/BrakeRequested. Then next: if OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ -> Release/Traversing. Does Released matter? No, separate. So if OR and Body, action Release, next Traversing. Need priority after AtGoal&Released. Does it apply even if AtGoal true but Released false? \"For inputs not handled so far\" includes AtGoal true but Released false, yes then OR&Body -> Release/Traversing if OR and body. But wait AtGoal alone not handled; if robot at goal but not released, maybe should Release? The policy says a robot that owns reservation and body clear must report Release/Traversing. Yes.\n\n\"For the remaining inputs, first retain Brake/BrakeRequested if the current mode is BrakeRequested and stop confirmation is still absent. If this does not apply, require Request/Waiting when ownership and released status are both absent. If that also does not apply, a Stopped robot takes Resume/ResumePending. Every input left over takes Proceed/Traversing.\"\n\nNeed order:\nAfter R3/R4/R5 (Release):\nR6 when ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted -> Brake, BrakeRequested.\nR7? when not OwnReservation and not Released -> Request, Waiting.\nR8? when ModeStopped -> Resume, ResumePending.\nR9 True -> Proceed, Traversing.\n\nNeed ensure priority. But wait there may be nuances: \"For remaining inputs, first retain Brake/BrakeRequested if current mode is BrakeRequested and stop confirmation is still absent. If this does not apply, require Request/Waiting when ownership and released status are both absent. If that also does not apply, a Stopped robot takes Resume/ResumePending. Every input left over takes Proceed/Traversing.\" This seems simple.\n\nNeed check all combinations and interactions with earlier rules. Need ensure first three check failure always Brake regardless of other conditions. Our R1/R2 use condition `not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked`. Note if PedestrianBlocked true, \"crossing is free of pedestrian block\" false, so Brake. Good.\n\nPotential issue: AtGoal+Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, then Finish/Done. If Halted true, action Finish/Done. If Halted false, Brake/BrakeRequested. We set R3 Halted -> Finish Done. R4 AtGoal and Released -> Brake BrakeRequested. But if Halted true, R3 first. If Halted false, R4. Good. But what if current mode Done and AtGoal Released Halted true? Rule R3 returns Finish/Done. Policy says no terminal shortcut, requirements apply in every mode including Done. AtGoal together Released requires Finish/Done after Halted. Even if already Done, returns Finish/Done. Good.\n\nPotential issue: Release/Traversing rule: \"For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing.\" Should this apply even if Released true? It says clear body and released separate. If OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ, report Release/Traversing. It doesn't say not Released. But \"For inputs not handled so far\" after AtGoal & Released. If Released true but not AtGoal, and OR & body, then Release/Traversing. Is that correct? Let's parse. \"Only when all three checks succeed does the dispatcher consider a released robot at its goal. AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done. For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing. Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\" Hmm \"released robot at its goal\" means AtGoal & Released. If Released is true and AtGoal false, and OR & Body, then it should Release/Traversing? But if it already has Released true, why release again? The phrase \"Clear body and released reservation are separate facts\" maybe means you don't need Released to be false to report Release when body clear and owns reservation. It might be weird: if Released true, OwnReservation true, Body clear, not AtGoal, policy says Release/Traversing because OR & Body. But could be intended: released reservation is an acknowledged release for its task; body clear means whole body clear controlled zone. They are separate. \"For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing.\" It doesn't mention Released. So yes. But let's think if \"released reservation\" means Released is true after release acknowledged. OwnReservation denotes this robot's ownership. Could both be true? Maybe not physically, but evaluator uses all combinations. They explicitly say don't infer relationships. So need handle inconsistent. Policy normative includes inconsistent-looking facts. It says \"Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\" So our rule should trigger on OR and Body regardless of Released. Good.\n\nNeed consider if AtGoal & Released and Halted false: R4 Brake/BrakeRequested. But if OR & Body also true, R4 before Release, so Brake. Correct because AtGoal+Released priority before Release. If AtGoal & Released and Halted true: R3 Finish/Done, even if OR&Body. Correct.\n\nNow after Release rule, remaining inputs are safe and not (AtGoal&Released) and not (OR&Body). Then R6 BrakeRequested and not Halted. Then R7 no ownership and no released. Then R8 ModeStopped. Then R9 Proceed.\n\nNeed check interactions: If OR true, Body false, Released false, ModeBrakeRequested false: R7? no because OR true. R8 maybe if stopped. Else Proceed. Policy says remaining: if ownership and released both absent -> Request/Waiting. Here ownership present, so not request. If stopped -> Resume. Else Proceed. Is that correct? Let's infer policy: After Release, remaining inputs. \"first retain Brake/BrakeRequested if current mode is BrakeRequested and stop confirmation absent. If this does not apply, require Request/Waiting when ownership and released status are both absent. If that also does not apply, a Stopped robot takes Resume/ResumePending. Every input left over takes Proceed/Traversing.\" So yes.\n\nBut wait: What about if current mode Stopped and ownership/released absent, after R7 Request/Waiting would take precedence over Resume. Policy says Request/Waiting before Stopped resume. So if no ownership and no released, even if ModeStopped, action Request/Waiting. Is that intended? \"If that also does not apply, a Stopped robot takes Resume/ResumePending.\" Yes. So R7 before R8. Good.\n\nWhat about if current mode BrakeRequested, not Halted, and no ownership no released? R6 Brake/BrakeRequested before Request. Correct.\n\nWhat about if current mode BrakeRequested, Halted true, and no ownership no released? R6 false due not H. R7 Request/Waiting. Policy: \"retain Brake/BrakeRequested if current mode BrakeRequested and stop confirmation is still absent. If this does not apply, require Request/Waiting when ownership and released status are both absent.\" If Halted true, stop confirmation present, so not retain; then request. Good.\n\nWhat about unsafe check and Halted? R1/R2 catch before all. Good.\n\nNeed consider Mode flags and step actions. We can use `ModeBrakeRequested` etc. Need expressions at most 512 chars, depth 8, nodes 64. Our expressions simple. Need exactly one JSON object. Need validate syntax: allowed names. For not, and, or. Use Python Boolean precedence. Parentheses okay. Need no comparisons. True/False allowed. We can use True in final default. Need action strings exact. Next mode strings exact without Mode prefix.\n\nNeed ensure at most 12 rules each. We have select maybe 7 rules, step 9 rules. Good.\n\nBut let's not rush. Need verify policy precisely. The prompt says \"Policy presentation P3: dispatcher's decision procedure.\" Wait could P3 refer only to step? The initial part includes select inputs. There may be separate policy for select? The text after step: \"Reservation selection has precisely the constraints given in the interface; no extra preference-following requirement is added.\" That implies select policy is just interface constraints. Need include select function. We must ensure select rules satisfy. Our select rules: \n1 when not OwnerFree -> Defer\n2 when RequestA and not RequestB -> SelectA\n3 when RequestB and not RequestA -> SelectB\n4 when RequestA and RequestB and PreferA and not PreferB -> SelectA\n5 when RequestA and RequestB and PreferB and not PreferA -> SelectB\n6 when RequestA and RequestB -> SelectA\n7 when True -> Defer\n\nCheck all cases. OwnerFree true, both requests true, PreferB true, PreferA false: rule5 SelectB. Legal because both eligible. OwnerFree true, both true, PreferA true, PreferB false: rule4 SelectA. OwnerFree true, both true, PreferA true, PreferB true: rule6 SelectA. Legal. OwnerFree true, both true, both false: rule6 SelectA. Legal. OwnerFree true, RequestA true, RequestB false, PreferB true: rule2 SelectA. Legal because B not eligible. OwnerFree true, RequestA false, RequestB true, PreferA true: rule3 SelectB. Legal. OwnerFree true, no requests: rule7 Defer. OwnerFree false, any requests: rule1 Defer. Requirement: If reservation free and at least one request exists, select eligible robot, rather than Defer. OwnerFree false, not free, Defer required. Yes.\n\nCould there be issue with rule 7 True -> Defer: If OwnerFree true and RequestA false RequestB false. Good. If OwnerFree false, rule1 earlier. If some weird names? No.\n\nNow step rules. Let's write exact expressions. Need decide whether to include safe condition in R3 onwards. Since R1/R2 catch unsafe, later can omit. But for readability and to ensure if first rules are true for unsafe, okay. However if we omit safe, then R3 might be true for unsafe and Halted? But R1 first if unsafe and Halted. R2 first if unsafe and not Halted. So R3 not reached. That's fine. But independent evaluator may check first true; order ensures. Could there be a case unsafe and Halted false where R2 true, so later irrelevant. Good.\n\nLet's define R1:\nwhen \"Halted and (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked)\" -> Brake, Stopped.\nR2:\nwhen \"not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked\" -> Brake, BrakeRequested.\nNeed check precedence: `Halted and (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked)`. Due `and` higher than `or`, parentheses. Good. R2 expression same without H. If H true, R1 true. If H false, R2 true. Good.\n\nR3:\nwhen \"AtGoal and Released and Halted\" -> Finish, Done.\nR4:\nwhen \"AtGoal and Released\" -> Brake, BrakeRequested.\nWait R4 if Halted true would also be true, but R3 first. If Halted false, R4 true. If Halted true and R3 true, R4 not reached. Good.\nBut what if unsafe and AtGoal Released Halted true? R1 catches. Good.\n\nR5:\nwhen \"OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ\" -> Release, Traversing.\nThis after R3/R4. If AtGoal Released Halted false, R4 catches before R5. If AtGoal Released Halted true, R3 catches. Good.\nQuestion: Should R5 include safe? If unsafe and OR&Body, R1/R2 catch. Good.\n\nR6:\nwhen \"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\" -> Brake, BrakeRequested.\nR7:\nwhen \"not OwnReservation and not Released\" -> Request, Waiting.\nR8:\nwhen \"ModeStopped\" -> Resume, ResumePending.\nR9:\nwhen \"True\" -> Proceed, Traversing.\n\nNeed check policy: \"AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done.\" Wait \"until Halted\" could mean if AtGoal and Released:\n- If Halted is false: Brake/BrakeRequested.\n- If Halted is true: Finish/Done.\nYes.\n\nPotential subtlety: \"Stop confirmation then decides the next mode: Halted means Stopped, and no Halted means BrakeRequested.\" This applies to first check failure. But for AtGoal+Released, \"requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done.\" So if Halted true, action Finish, next Done, not Brake/Stopped. We set.\n\nPotential subtlety: First check failure: \"If any of these three checks fails, its instruction is Brake. Stop confirmation then decides the next mode: Halted means Stopped, and no Halted means BrakeRequested.\" So if ObservationUsable false but TaskActive false? Brake. Yes.\n\nPotential subtlety: \"At every update dispatcher first asks whether observation can be used, task remains active, and crossing free of pedestrian block. If any of these three checks fails, its instruction is Brake. Stop confirmation then decides the next mode: Halted means Stopped, and no Halted means BrakeRequested. No other instruction takes precedence over this instruction.\" So if Halted true and unsafe, next Stopped. If Halted false, next BrakeRequested. Good.\n\nPotential subtlety: \"Only when all three checks succeed does the dispatcher consider a released robot at its goal. AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done.\" Could there be a case safe, AtGoal and Released and Halted true but current mode Done? We return Finish/Done. Yes.\n\nPotential subtlety: \"For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing.\" Does \"not handled so far\" include safe? Yes. Our R5 doesn't include safe but safe is guaranteed by prior. Good.\n\nPotential subtlety: \"For the remaining inputs, first retain Brake/BrakeRequested if the current mode is BrakeRequested and stop confirmation is still absent.\" \"stop confirmation is still absent\" means Halted false. We use not Halted. Good.\n\" If this does not apply, require Request/Waiting when ownership and released status are both absent.\" We use not OR and not Released. \"ownership and released status are both absent\" means OwnReservation false and Released false. Yes.\n\"If that also does not apply, a Stopped robot takes Resume/ResumePending.\" We use ModeStopped. \"Every input left over takes Proceed/Traversing.\" True.\n\nNeed ensure expressions don't use mode names in select and step not use select names. We don't.\n\nNeed ensure JSON UTF-8, no Markdown. Root exactly three keys. Schema value. select and step arrays. Each select rule exactly when and action. Step rule exactly when, action, next. We must not include extra fields. Need duplicate keys no. We'll produce compact JSON. Need maybe escape nothing. We can use newlines? The instruction says exactly one UTF-8 JSON object as complete response, without Markdown fences, explanatory prose, comments, or additional fields. Whitespace okay. We can output pretty JSON? It's still one JSON object. To be safe, compact or pretty with no comments. Pretty is fine. But maybe exactly one JSON object. We'll output raw JSON. Ensure no trailing commas. Need check all strings valid.\n\nLet's consider whether to include `True` in final default. Allowed True. Yes.\n\nNow, before final, let's deeply examine if there is any hidden requirement about \"at most 32 total branches are allowed; per-function limit 12 still applies.\" What are branches? Probably number of rules? At most 32 total rules across select+step? \"At most 32 total branches are allowed; the per-function limit of twelve still applies.\" Our total rules 7+9=16, fine.\n\nNeed ensure condition has at most 512 chars, 64 AST nodes, depth 8. Our longest R1: `Halted and (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked)` length ~77. Nodes: Halted (1), and (1), not (1), ObservationUsable (1), or (1), not (1), TaskActive (1), or (1), PedestrianBlocked (1) plus parentheses not nodes? Python AST: BoolOp And, Name, BoolOp Or, UnaryOp Not, Name, UnaryOp Not, Name, Name. Nodes ~10. Depth maybe 4. Good.\n\nSelect expressions okay.\n\nPotential issue: \"Expressions allow only True, False, the permitted Boolean names, not, and, or, and parentheses, with Python Boolean precedence. No calls, comparisons, arithmetic, attributes, new names, or assignment.\" Our expressions use `not`, `and`, `or`, parentheses. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"A step expression may also refer to ModeIdle, ModeWaiting, ModeTraversing, ModeBrakeRequested, ModeStopped, ModeResumePending, ModeDone. Exactly one such mode flag is true. select cannot use any step or mode names; step cannot use any select names.\" Our step uses ModeBrakeRequested, ModeStopped. Good. Our select uses only select names.\n\nPotential issue: \"Every possible input must have a result.\" Our final True covers. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"First rule whose expression evaluates to true determines the result.\" Our order normative. Need ensure select rule order: If OwnerFree false and RequestA true, rule1 Defer. Good. If OwnerFree true and RequestA true RequestB false, rule2 SelectA. Good. If OwnerFree true and RequestA false RequestB true, rule3 SelectB. Good. If OwnerFree true and both true and PreferA true PreferB false, rule4 SelectA. If both true and PreferB true PreferA false, rule5 SelectB. If both true and both true, rule6 SelectA. If both true and both false, rule6 SelectA. If OwnerFree true and no requests, rule6 false, rule7 Defer. Good.\n\nCould there be a case OwnerFree true and RequestA true RequestB false but PreferB true? Rule2 SelectA. Legal. Preference doesn't impose obligation. Good.\n\nNow step rules order. Let's simulate some cases to ensure no conflicts.\n\nCase unsafe (OU false or TA false or PB true). R1 if H true -> Brake/Stopped. R2 if H false -> Brake/BrakeRequested. This overrides everything. Good.\n\nCase safe, AtGoal true, Released true:\n- H true: R1 false (safe), R2 false, R3 true -> Finish/Done.\n- H false: R3 false, R4 true -> Brake/BrakeRequested.\nIf also OR&Body, R5 not reached. Good.\n\nCase safe, not (AtGoal&Released), OR&Body:\n- If AtGoal true Released false, R3/R4 false. R5 true -> Release/Traversing.\n- If AtGoal false Released true, R5 true -> Release/Traversing. Is that correct? Let's re-read: \"Only when all three checks succeed does the dispatcher consider a released robot at its goal. AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done. For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing. Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\" Suppose Released true (acknowledged release) but AtGoal false. \"For inputs not handled so far\" yes. It owns reservation and body clear -> Release/Traversing. Even though already released? The policy might expect that if Released true and not AtGoal, then maybe Request? Wait let's parse full sequence.\n\nMaybe the mode progression:\n- Initially Idle, no reservation. Request/Waiting.\n- OwnReservation true and BodyClear? Actually \"a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing.\" This seems action Release next Traversing. That is odd: if it owns reservation and body clear, it releases reservation while traversing? Maybe this is for when it has completed crossing and is clear of zone, it should release reservation. Next mode Traversing? Wait action Release, next Traversing. That seems if it owns reservation and body clear, release and go Traversing. Hmm.\n\nLet's reconstruct policy from P3 maybe for robot crossing:\n- If observation unusable, task inactive, pedestrian blocked: Brake.\n- If at goal and released: Finish/Done after Halted.\n- If owns reservation and body clear of zone: Release/Traversing. Wait action Release and next Traversing? That seems contradictory: if body clear, why Traversing? Maybe \"report Release/Traversing\" means action Release, next mode Traversing. Could be: while traversing, when body clear of zone, release reservation but continue traversing? Actually Traversing mode might mean in corridor? Hmm.\n\n- Remaining: if ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted: Brake/BrakeRequested.\n- if no ownership and no released: Request/Waiting.\n- if ModeStopped: Resume/ResumePending.\n- else Proceed/Traversing.\n\nThis is a mode supervisor. Not our job to interpret physical.\n\nBut need ensure \"Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\" That explicitly says Released separate from BodyClearOfZ. So our rule using OR&Body regardless of Released is likely intended.\n\nLet's check if there is any condition \"a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing.\" If Released true, it doesn't own? Actually OwnReservation denotes this robot's ownership. Released denotes an acknowledged reservation release for its task. They could both be true in inconsistent input. Policy says handle inconsistent-looking facts normatively. Since rule condition doesn't include not Released, yes.\n\nCase safe, not (AtGoal&Released), not (OR&Body). Then R6 if ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted -> Brake/BrakeRequested.\nThen R7 if not OR and not Released -> Request/Waiting.\nThen R8 if ModeStopped -> Resume/ResumePending.\nThen R9 Proceed/Traversing.\n\nConsider safe, ModeStopped, OR false, Released false. R7 true -> Request/Waiting, not Resume. Policy: Request/Waiting when ownership and released absent. If that applies, it takes precedence over Stopped resume. Yes.\n\nConsider safe, ModeStopped, OR true, Released false, Body false. R5 false. R6 maybe false. R7 false (OR true). R8 true -> Resume/ResumePending. Policy: remaining, first BrakeRequested absent, Request if ownership/released both absent. Here ownership present, so not. Stopped -> Resume. Yes.\n\nConsider safe, ModeStopped, OR false, Released true, Body false. R5 false. R6 false. R7 false (Released true). R8 true -> Resume/ResumePending. Policy: ownership and released both absent? No, Released present. So not Request. Stopped -> Resume. Yes.\n\nConsider safe, ModeBrakeRequested, Halted true, OR false, Released false. R6 false because Halted. R7 true -> Request/Waiting. Policy: \"retain Brake/BrakeRequested if current mode is BrakeRequested and stop confirmation is still absent.\" Halted true means stop confirmation present, so don't retain. Then Request/Waiting if no ownership/released. Yes.\n\nConsider safe, ModeBrakeRequested, Halted true, OR true, Released true, AtGoal false, Body false. R3/R4 false? AtGoal false. R5 false body. R6 false H. R7 false. R8 false (not Stopped). R9 Proceed/Traversing. Policy: remaining, not retain Brake, not Request (ownership/released not both absent), not Stopped, left over Proceed. Yes.\n\nConsider safe, ModeBrakeRequested, Halted false, OR true, Released true, AtGoal false, Body false. R6 true -> Brake/BrakeRequested. Policy: first retain Brake/BrakeRequested if current mode BrakeRequested and stop confirmation absent. Yes, even if ownership/released present. Good.\n\nConsider safe, AtGoal true, Released true, Halted false. R4 Brake/BrakeRequested. Even if ModeStopped? R4 before R8. Policy: AtGoal+Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted. Yes.\n\nConsider safe, AtGoal true, Released true, Halted true. R3 Finish/Done. Good.\n\nNow, is R1/R2 condition correct? \"At every update the dispatcher first asks whether the observation can be used, the task remains active, and the crossing is free of a pedestrian block. If any of these three checks fails, its instruction is Brake.\" The checks: observation can be used = ObservationUsable; task remains active = TaskActive; crossing free of pedestrian block = not PedestrianBlocked. If any fails = not OU or not TA or PB. Yes.\n\nNeed consider if `PedestrianBlocked` denotes a blocked crossing. So crossing free = not PedestrianBlocked. Yes.\n\nNow, maybe \"Stop confirmation then decides the next mode: Halted means Stopped, and no Halted means BrakeRequested.\" In R1/R2, if Halted true -> next Stopped. If Halted false -> next BrakeRequested. Yes.\n\nNow, let's think about \"No special terminal-mode shortcut exists: the requirements apply in every mode, including Done.\" Our rules don't have any mode-specific terminal shortcut except R8 uses ModeStopped, R6 ModeBrakeRequested. ModeDone doesn't bypass anything. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"At most twelve rules in each function.\" We have 9 step, 7 select. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"At most 32 total branches are allowed.\" 16 rules. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"Source size at most 200 * 1024 bytes.\" tiny.\n\nPotential issue: \"The root has exactly three keys: schema, select, step.\" We must not include other keys. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"Both functions