not RequestA\", \"action\": \"SelectB\"},\n    {\"when\": \"RequestA and RequestB and PreferA and not PreferB\", \"action\": \"SelectA\"},\n    {\"when\": \"RequestA and RequestB and PreferB and not PreferA\", \"action\": \"SelectB\"},\n    {\"when\": \"RequestA and RequestB\", \"action\": \"SelectA\"},\n    {\"when\": \"True\", \"action\": \"Defer\"}\n  ],\n  \"step\": [\n    {\"when\": \"Halted and (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked)\", \"action\": \"Brake\", \"next\": \"Stopped\"},\n    {\"when\": \"not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked\", \"action\": \"Brake\", \"next\": \"BrakeRequested\"},\n    {\"when\": \"AtGoal and Released and Halted\", \"action\": \"Finish\", \"next\": \"Done\"},\n    {\"when\": \"AtGoal and Released\", \"action\": \"Brake\", \"next\": \"BrakeRequested\"},\n    {\"when\": \"OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ\", \"action\": \"Release\", \"next\": \"Traversing\"},\n    {\"when\": \"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\", \"action\": \"Brake\", \"next\": \"BrakeRequested\"},\n    {\"when\": \"not OwnReservation and not Released\", \"action\": \"Request\", \"next\": \"Waiting\"},\n    {\"when\": \"ModeStopped\", \"action\": \"Resume\", \"next\": \"ResumePending\"},\n    {\"when\": \"True\", \"action\": \"Proceed\", \"next\": \"Traversing\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nNeed check if R5 should perhaps be before R4? No, policy says AtGoal+Released before Release. Good.\n\nNow, let's double-check if select rule 6 `RequestA and RequestB` after rule 4/5. If both requests true, PreferA false PreferB true, rule5 selects B. If both requests true, PreferA true PreferB true, rule6 selects A. If both requests true, PreferA false PreferB false, rule6 selects A. If both requests true, PreferA true PreferB false, rule4 selects A. If both requests true, PreferA false PreferB false, rule6 A. Legal. But does this violate \"preferences do not impose an additional acceptance obligation\"? No. It just uses preference to break tie. The p